---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import { doc } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['lanes', 'Lanes and the home screen'],
  ['branches', 'Branches and worktrees'],
  ['composer', 'Talk to the agent'],
  ['review', 'Review what it did'],
  ['models', 'Models, thinking, and context'],
  ['merge', 'Pull requests and merging'],
  ['subagents', 'Split work across agents'],
  ['artifacts', 'Artifacts'],
  ['history', 'Resume, rewind, and fork'],
  ['routines', 'Routines'],
  ['skills', 'Skills'],
  ['settings', 'Settings worth knowing'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Daily work"
  eyebrow="Guide / using Phosphor"
  description="Everything after setup: run several lanes, steer the agent, review and merge its work, and use artifacts, routines, and skills. Each section links to its reference."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="lanes">
    <h2>Lanes and the home screen</h2>
    <p>
      Every chat is a lane: one agent, usually on its own branch and worktree. The sidebar lists
      lanes by project, with their status, branch, and a PR chip once there’s a pull request.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Home</strong> (New in the sidebar) sorts the lanes that need something into columns: Waiting
        on you, Ready to merge, Needs a push, In review, and Running. Below that, a ledger shows what
        the project has spent, its tokens, and how much memory the live lanes use.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Find a lane</strong> with the magnifier in a project’s header. It matches title, branch,
        and PR number.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Right-click a lane</strong> to pin it, give it a marker, open its pull request, suspend
        it to free memory, fork or clone it, export it as HTML, copy its spend, or delete it. Double-click
        to rename. Drag rows to reorder them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('lanes.md')}>Lanes reference</a> · <a href={doc('ui-shell.md')}
        >Sidebar and window</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="branches">
    <h2>Branches and worktrees</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>new branch</strong> box under the home composer decides whether a chat gets its own
      branch and worktree. It’s on by default and branches off trunk. Turn it off for a quick question,
      or to keep working on the branch that’s already checked out.
    </p>
    <p>
      The branch control in the top bar searches branches, switches between the main checkout and
      worktrees, creates a worktree from a base you pick, pulls trunk, and updates a worktree from
      trunk when it falls behind. Deleting a lane that’s alone in its worktree offers to remove the
      worktree too.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('worktrees.md')}>Worktrees reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="composer">
    <h2>Talk to the agent</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>While it works:</strong> Enter queues a steering message, delivered after the current
        tool calls. Option+Enter (Alt+Enter) queues a follow-up for when it finishes. Esc stops the turn.
      </li><li><strong>@</strong> searches the workspace and adds a file reference.</li><li>
        <strong>Images:</strong> paste or drag them in. Click one to mark it up with boxes, arrows, and
        text before sending.
      </li><li>
        <strong>!command</strong> runs a shell command and adds its output to the context. <strong
          >!!command</strong
        > runs it without sending the output to the model.
      </li><li>
        <strong>/</strong> lists commands from pi, your prompt templates, and your extensions.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Rules · MCP</strong> next to the attach button turns CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md on or off
        for the lane, and enables or disables MCP servers.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Drafts, attachments included, survive switching lanes and quitting. Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+X opens a
      taller field for long prompts.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('chat.md#composer')}>Composer reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>Review what it did</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The transcript:</strong> each tool call is a row you can expand for its arguments and
        output. Edits open as diffs. Cmd/Ctrl+F searches the whole session, tool output included.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Changes:</strong> every file the lane changed, with its diff. Revert puts a file’s working
        copy back.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Files:</strong> an explorer and an editor with tabs, search, and save.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Terminal:</strong> tabbed terminals for the lane’s folder.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Panes open from the top bar, one at a time beside the chat. Drag the divider, dock the pane
      left or right, or make it fullscreen. Each lane remembers its own layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('files.md')}>Files and editor</a> · <a href={doc('terminal.md')}>Terminal</a> · <a
        href={doc('chat.md#tool-renderers')}>Tool cards</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="models">
    <h2>Models, thinking, and context</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Model menu:</strong> every model you’re signed into, searchable, with stars and recents.
        Switch mid-session and the conversation carries over.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Thinking:</strong> pick the level for the next turn, from off to max, as far as the model
        supports.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Context meter:</strong> how full the context is, tokens and cost, an estimate of what’s
        filling it, and your Claude or ChatGPT plan usage.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Compaction:</strong> sessions compact at a shared context budget, 200k tokens by default.
        Change it in Settings → Agent, or compact now from the lane’s ⋮ menu.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Running low on a plan? <a href="/guides/accounts/#pivot">Move the lane to another one</a>. <a
        href={doc('chat.md#what-the-context-meters-popover-shows')}>Context meter reference</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="merge">
    <h2>Pull requests and merging</h2>
    <p>
      A worktree lane is told to commit on its branch and open a pull request when the work is done.
      Phosphor itself only reads PR status, through the gh CLI: number, state, checks, and review.
      It never pushes or opens a PR for you.
    </p>
    <p>
      To merge, use <strong>Merge</strong> on a Ready to merge card, the branch control, or the project
      menu. The merge runs locally with git and walks you through a dirty worktree first. A worktree lane
      that has fallen behind main shows <strong>Update</strong> under Needs a push.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('lanes.md#pr-status')}>PR status reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="subagents">
    <h2>Split work across agents</h2>
    <p>
      With the <a href="/guides/extensions/#subagents">Subagents package</a> installed, ask the agent
      to split independent work. Each child shows in the chat with its current tool, its last few steps,
      and its result. Background runs stay in a strip under the chat, and the lane keeps showing as working
      until they finish.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('chat.md#sub-agents')}>Sub-agents reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="artifacts">
    <h2>Artifacts</h2>
    <p>
      Ask for a plan, a doc, a diagram, a chart, or an HTML page as an artifact. It opens in the
      Artifacts pane next to the chat and keeps every version, even after the session compacts. The
      Artifacts page in the sidebar lists every artifact from every session. HTML artifacts run
      isolated, with no network access.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('extensions.md#the-artifact-tools-and-what-each-one-costs')}
        >Artifacts reference</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="history">
    <h2>Resume, rewind, and fork</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Old sessions reopen from the sidebar with their full history.</li><li>
        <strong>Rewind to here</strong> on one of your messages, or Esc Esc for a picker, branches the
        chat from just before it and puts your text back in the composer. The old version folds away.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Fork</strong> and <strong>Clone</strong> in a lane’s menu make a copy and keep both.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={doc('lanes.md#forks-and-saved-history')}>Forks and saved history</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="routines">
    <h2>Routines</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Routines</strong> in the sidebar runs a task on a schedule: hourly, daily, weekly, monthly,
      once, or a cron expression. Each run starts a fresh lane with the model you picked. Routines run
      on your machine while it’s awake, not in the cloud. <strong>Make routine…</strong> in a lane’s menu
      drafts one from that lane.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('routines.md')}>Routines reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="skills">
    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <p>
      A skill is a written procedure the agent can follow. The Skills page in the sidebar lists your
      own and the ones your packages bring. Create, import, or export your own there, or add a
      reviewed one from discovery.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('ui-shell.md#global-surfaces')}>Skills reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="settings">
    <h2>Settings worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Keyboard:</strong> Cmd/Ctrl+K opens the command palette, Cmd/Ctrl+N starts a session,
        and Cmd/Ctrl+B toggles the sidebar. Settings → Keybindings lists the rest.
      </li><li><strong>Appearance:</strong> light, dark, or follow the system.</li><li>
        <strong>Agent → Directives:</strong> what Phosphor adds to every lane’s system prompt, shown in
        full before it’s sent.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Optimization:</strong> Headroom, an optional local proxy that restructures large JSON
        tool results before they enter the context. Its Advisor reads your session files and settings
        and points out what’s worth changing.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Advanced → Maintenance:</strong> finds worktrees of lanes that already landed and reports
        how much space they hold. It deletes them only if you ask.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Send feedback</strong> at the bottom of the sidebar opens a prefilled GitHub issue with
        your rating and notes. Nothing about your code or sessions is attached.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={doc('settings.md')}>Settings reference</a></p>
  </section>
</Guide>
